description: UI spec structure, hooks, steps, tags, and spec helper policy
always sortingalwaysApply: true
Filter products by brand
Submit checkout form
Verify order confirmation is shown
```

### Bad step names

```text
Click button
Fill input
Wait for table
Check element
Call method
```

### Rule

If a step name would not make sense to a product owner, QA reviewer, or support engineer, it is probably too technical.

---

## Hooks

- `beforeEach` may only perform safe navigation and page-loaded checks.
- `beforeEach` must not perform the action under test.
- `beforeEach` must not hide business flows.
- `beforeEach` must not silently change application state unless the state is generic setup required for every test in the suite.
- Do not repeat navigation already done in `beforeEach`.
- Keep navigation-path tests in a separate `describe` block when needed.
- Hook logic must stay small, explicit, and easy to reason about.

### Good

```ts
test.describe("Product sorting", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@sorting"] }, () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ homePage }) => {
    await homePage.open();
    await homePage.expectLoaded();
  });

  test("sorts products by price", async ({ homePage }) => {
    await test.step("Apply ascending price sorting", async () => {
      await homePage.sorting.applySort("price,asc");
    });

    await test.step("Verify products are sorted by price", async () => {
      await expectSortedByPrice(await homePage.productList.getVisiblePrices(), "asc");
    });
  });
});
```

### Bad

```ts
test.describe("Product sorting", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@sorting"] }, () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ homePage }) => {
    await homePage.open();
    await homePage.sorting.applySort("price,asc");
  });

  test("sorts products by price", async ({ homePage }) => {
    await expect(homePage.productList.items).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

### Why this is bad

The sorting action is the behavior under test, but it is hidden inside `beforeEach`.

The test body no longer clearly shows what scenario is being verified.

---

## Authentication Setup

- Do not perform UI login in `beforeEach` unless the suite explicitly tests login or navigation behavior.
- For pre-authenticated UI tests, prefer storage state, API setup, or a project-level authenticated context.
- Core framework may provide authentication mechanisms.
- Concrete users, roles, credentials, and login flows must stay in the project layer.

### Bad

```ts
test.beforeEach(async ({ loginPage, user }) => {
  await loginPage.open();
  await loginPage.login(user);
});
```

This is bad when the suite does not test login behavior.

### Better

```ts
test.describe("Authenticated product area", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@auth"] }, () => {
  test.use({ storageState: "state/authenticated-user.json" });

  test("shows user-specific products", async ({ homePage }) => {
    await test.step("Open product catalog", async () => {
      await homePage.open();
      await homePage.expectLoaded();
    });
  });
});
```

---

## Navigation Rules

- If `beforeEach` opens a page, tests in that `describe` block should not repeat the same navigation.
- If a test verifies navigation itself, it should not reuse a `beforeEach` that already navigates to the destination.
- Navigation-path tests should be grouped separately.

### Good

```ts
test.describe("Product page content", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@catalog"] }, () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ productPage }) => {
    await productPage.open();
    await productPage.expectLoaded();
  });

  test("shows product title", async ({ productPage }) => {
    await test.step("Verify product title is shown", async () => {
      await expect(productPage.title).toBeVisible();
    });
  });
});
```

### Good for navigation behavior

```ts
test.describe("Product navigation", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@catalog"] }, () => {
  test("opens product details from catalog", async ({ catalogPage, productPage }) => {
    await test.step("Open product catalog", async () => {
      await catalogPage.open();
      await catalogPage.expectLoaded();
    });

    await test.step("Open first product details", async () => {
      await catalogPage.productList.openFirstProduct();
    });

    await test.step("Verify product details page is shown", async () => {
      await expect(productPage.title).toBeVisible();
    });
  });
});
```

